### Pre-Test vs Post-Test Iteration

In Java, when the number of iterations cannot be known in advance (such as reading user input until they type "exit", waiting for a server response, or draining an account balance), `while` and `do-while` loops are the natural choice.

The crucial architectural distinction between them is **when the condition is tested**:

---

### 1. The `while` Loop (Pre-Test)

A `while` loop checks its condition **first**, before running any code:

```java
while (condition) {
    // Body executes only if condition is true
}
```

If the condition is `false` initially, the body **executes zero times**:

```java
int balance = 0;
while (balance > 0) {
    System.out.println("Dispensing cash..."); // NEVER EXECUTES!
}
```

---

### 2. The `do-while` Loop (Post-Test)

A `do-while` loop executes the body **first**, and tests the condition **at the very bottom**:

```java
do {
    // Body executes AT LEAST ONCE unconditionally
} while (condition); // Note the mandatory semicolon!
```

Even if the condition is `false` immediately, the body **guarantees at least one execution**:

```java
int balance = 0;
do {
    System.out.println("Processing transaction..."); // EXECUTES ONCE!
} while (balance > 0);
```

Notice the terminating semicolon `;` after `while (condition);` — omitting this semicolon causes a compile error.

---

### The Classic Use Case: Interactive Menus

`do-while` is universally used for console menu loops. You want to display the menu and prompt the user for a choice at least once, repeating only if they choose to continue:

```java
int choice;
do {
    System.out.println("1. Check Balance | 2. Deposit | 3. Exit");
    choice = getUserChoice();
} while (choice != 3);
```

| Feature | `while` Loop | `do-while` Loop |
|---|---|---|
| Evaluation Timing | Pre-test (at entry) | Post-test (at exit) |
| Minimum Executions | **0** | **1** |
| Syntax Semicolon | No semicolon after condition | **Mandatory semicolon** `;` |
| Primary Use Case | Condition-based data processing | Menus, prompts, retries |

activities.codeSnippet:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // while demo: condition checked first
        int counter = 10;
        System.out.println("Starting while loop (counter=10, condition < 5):");
        while (counter < 5) {
            System.out.println("while body running!"); // Never runs
        }

        // do-while demo: body runs before check
        System.out.println("Starting do-while loop (counter=10, condition < 5):");
        do {
            System.out.println("do-while body executed at least once!");
            counter++;
        } while (counter < 5);

        System.out.println("Comparison complete.");
    }
}

